ned a cohort  of several hundred  outbred cd1s for aggression over three  days of resident intruder testing what  we found is about  70 percent of any given cohort of cd1s  exhibited some level of aggression  across three days  but what was really interesting is that  if you look at the mean latency  over the three days between each of  those aggressive mice there's a really  beautiful spectrum  and so now this allows us to look at the  animals that have very low latency or  very high levels of aggression uh medium  level  or relatively weak aggression and of  course animals that fail to show any  aggression at all  so using this type of individual  variability we can start to ask what's  different  why are some extremely aggressive why  are some not aggressive at all  unfortunately with resident intruder  testing  you can't really differentiate between  reactive aggression and repetitive  aggression  or that is you know regression that's in  response to a threat that fight or  flight  response or aggression that's because an  animal finds it rewarding  because it's a forced encounter in their  home cage so  to get around that we needed to develop  a new method  that would allow us to isolate the  initiation of aggression  from the expression of reward and to do  that we used aggression  aggression condition place preference so  an aggression cpp  you have a three chamber apparatus a  chamber on the left a chamber in the  middle and a chamber on the right  and the middle chamber is neutral but  the left and right chambers have  significantly different contextual cues  so for example the size of the floor  grids  might be used the coloration of the  walls  the light gradient oftentimes labs will  use an olfactory cue that's different  between the two contexts  but the point here is that it's very  easy for  a a mouse to identify context a  and context b and so uh using this  type of apparatus we could pair one side  with a fight or an aggressive  interaction and the 